Jahshaka patch 0084: a warm-up set skips a material this process does not have,
instead of warming the DEFAULT datablock and calling it a success
(WARMUPSET-1, 2026-09-21; SOURCE: every tree re-runs build-ogre.sh).

WHY. VertexFormatWarmUpStorage records, per vertex format, one representative
MATERIAL NAME per Hlms hash, and createWarmUp applies those names to degenerate
renderables so the permutations compile before the window opens.
`Renderable::setDatablock( IdString )` resolves a name through
HlmsManager::getDatablock(), which for a name it cannot find logs LML_CRITICAL
and hands back the DEFAULT datablock. So a recorded material that is absent
from this process does not fail: it silently warms a DIFFERENT permutation --
one no draw in the session will use -- and the storage reports the compiles it
caused as a successful warm-up.

MEASURED on this host, whose datablock names are per-process counters
("pbr_18", "unlit_21", "outline_31" -- decoded from the recorded IdString
hashes): on EVERY warm launch, seven "Can't find HLMS datablock material" lines
and eight shaders compiled from the default datablock, none of them ever bound;
the next launch then loads those eight from the shader cache for ever. The
count the host prints ("replayed the recorded warm-up set (8 shaders built)")
was measuring the defect.

WHAT IT DOES. createWarmUp resolves every recorded name with
getDatablockNoDefault() FIRST. A missing one is skipped and counted; an entry
whose materials are ALL missing is skipped before its dummy buffers are built
(an entry that spawns nothing would leak its VAO and vertex buffers, which
destroyWarmUp reaches through a spawned renderable). Two consequences of the
skip are covered in the same hunk: destroyWarmUp takes its SceneManager from
ANY entry that spawned something rather than only the last one, and a call that
spawned nothing at all frees the dummy skeleton it may have created while it
still has a SceneManager to free it with. One summary log line replaces the
per-material CRITICAL spam, and it names the real precondition: a warm-up set
records a material by NAME, so names that are not stable across runs warm
nothing.

NOT FIXED HERE, and reported upward: with per-process names a Jahshaka warm-up
set can warm nothing at all. The sound fix is on the HOST side -- record the
recipe (the material's own parameters) beside the set and recreate those
datablocks before the replay, or give datablocks names that mean the same thing
in the next process.

